<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Three permissions, and the two that are missing matter as much as these.

      POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a runtime permission from API 33 up. Declaring it is
      not enough; MainActivity has to ask, and the person can say no.

      RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED is install-time. Without it BootReceiver never runs
      and every alarm dies at the next restart, silently.

      VIBRATE is install-time and uncontroversial. The channel is silent, so
      vibration is the only signal there is.

      SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is what lets a reminder arrive at the minute it was
      set for. It was deliberately left out at first, on the grounds that
      batched wake-ups are kinder to a tired battery — decided while the task
      list was four hardcoded lines, so the saving cost nothing visible. It
      turned out to cost a reminder arriving twenty-five minutes late, on a list
      that has medication in it. Eight timers firing on the minute is a fraction
      of a second of CPU each; what costs power on this path is the sync that
      runs inside the alarm, not the timer.

      It is a permission the person grants in system settings, not a pop-up, so
      the app must work without it: the scheduler falls back to the batched call
      and the home screen says reminders may be late, with a way to fix it.

      NOT declared: USE_EXACT_ALARM. It needs no granting, and Google restricts
      it to apps whose main purpose is an alarm clock or calendar. This is
      neither, so declaring it would risk the listing for a convenience.

      NOT declared: FOREGROUND_SERVICE. Nothing runs in the background at all.
      The app is asleep between the moment it is closed and the moment the OS
      wakes it — for an alarm, or, when slips are switched on, for a picture a
      bank's app has just saved (SlipWatch.kt), or, when money coming in is
      switched on, for a bank's notification (BankNoticeListener.kt).
    -->
    <!--
      INTERNET is install-time and was not here until sync existed, which is
      correct: an app that declares a permission it does not use is an app
      nobody can audit. It is here now because the sync screen talks to a WebDAV
      server. Without it every sync fails with a SecurityException while every
      line of code involved is right, which is a bad hour to spend.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />

    <!--
      The gallery, for slips that arrive on their own. Asked for only when that
      is switched on in Settings, and only the albums picked there are read.

      READ_MEDIA_IMAGES is the whole gallery from Android 13; before that the
      same thing was READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, which is capped at 32 so a newer
      phone is never asked for the old, wider one.

      READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED is declared so that "selected photos
      only" on Android 14 is a state the app can see and explain. Without it
      the system hands out a temporary grant instead, which looks like full
      access until the app is closed and then quietly is not. A slip saved
      tomorrow is never among the photos picked today, so that state is the
      one the settings screen has to be able to name.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />

    <!--
      usesCleartextTraffic, and the trade it represents.

      Android refuses plain http by default from API 28 up, with no per-network
      exception that can be written down in advance — the rules are per domain,
      and a NAS on a home network has an address that is not known until someone
      types it in. So the platform's answer here is all or nothing.

      The rule that actually applies is in Storage.kt and has tests: https to
      anywhere, http only to 127.x, 10.x, 172.16-31.x, 192.168.x, .local and
      localhost. That check runs before a request is built, so turning this flag
      on does not permit anything the app will actually do. What it buys is that
      rclone or a NAS on the LAN works during setup, instead of failing with a
      platform error that names none of this.
    -->
    <!--
      android:icon was never set, so every launcher has been drawing the
      platform default next to everything else on the home screen. The
      adaptive pair it points at has been sitting in res/drawable since the
      day the project was created, unreferenced.

      minSdk is 26, so mipmap-anydpi answers for every phone that can run
      this and the legacy webp files under mipmap-hdpi and friends are never
      read. They can be deleted whenever somebody feels like it.
    -->
    <!--
      A moving wallpaper is something the phone may not offer at all. Not
      required: without it the app is the same app, and the wallpaper row in
      Settings is the only thing that cannot be used.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.software.live_wallpaper"
        android:required="false" />

    <application
        android:label="Reup"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.NoActionBar"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
        android:allowBackup="false">

        <!--
          allowBackup="false" is here from the very first commit rather than as
          a later hardening step. Android's Auto Backup uploads app data to the
          user's Google Drive by default, which for this app would eventually
          mean a personal finance database and every task name leaving the
          device with nobody having asked for it. Easier to never turn it on
          than to remember to turn it off once there is data worth taking.
        -->

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Not exported: nothing outside the app sends here, and it holds the
          pairing code, which is the encryption key. An exported activity is a
          thing any installed app can start.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".SyncActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="ซิงก์กับคอม" />

        <!-- Adding a task. Not exported: nothing outside this app has any
             business writing rows into it. adjustResize for the same reason
             as the money form: its save button rides on the keyboard. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".AddTaskActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="เพิ่มงาน"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

        <!--
          Money, both directions. Not exported, same as the rest.
          adjustResize so the keyboard's height reaches the screen as an inset:
          the save button rides on top of the keyboard instead of under it.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".AddMoneyActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="บันทึกเงิน"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

        <!--
          The one exported component in this app, and the only one that has to
          be: a browser cannot hand a result back to an activity it is not
          allowed to start.

          Kept apart from SyncActivity on purpose. Putting the filter there
          would export the settings screen itself and change its launch mode for
          every other way in. Here the surface any app can reach is one file
          that reads a Uri and hands it to code that does not trust it.

          What protects it is not obscurity — the scheme is right here, and it
          can be derived from the client id anyway. It is that the redirect is
          checked against a state this app generated and stored before the
          browser opened, and against a verifier only this install holds.

          singleTask so coming back lands on the copy already running rather
          than stacking a second one behind the browser.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".OAuthRedirectActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.NoDisplay">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <!--
                  The client id backwards. Google reserves this scheme when the
                  Android client is created, so it is never typed into the
                  console — but it is typed here, and androidRedirectUri builds
                  the same string on the other side. A test compares the two,
                  because one wrong character produces a browser sitting on a
                  page nothing answers, with no error at either end.
                -->
                <data android:scheme="com.googleusercontent.apps.250966479972-6o0u00rso1aq0kko2rjr10kv8cdu1bhl" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The screen that shows what a recogniser read and what the rules made
          of it, side by side. Not exported: nothing outside this app has any
          business starting it, and it is a debugging surface before it is a
          feature.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ScanSlipActivity"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          A waiting slip's picture, whole, opened from its row in the money tab
          and from the form. Not exported, like every screen here that only
          this app opens.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".SlipPictureActivity"
            android:exported="false" />


        <!--
          Not exported: nothing outside the app ever sends to this. The
          PendingIntent the scheduler builds names the class explicitly, which
          works regardless.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".AlarmReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          The button in the notification shade. Not exported: the only thing
          that ever sends this is a PendingIntent this app built and handed to
          the system, and the system sends it back as this app. Exporting it
          would mean any installed app could tick tasks off by broadcasting a
          uid it guessed, which is a small thing to be able to do to somebody
          and no thing at all to gain.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".DoneReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- The save and don't-save buttons on a slip. Not exported, for the
             same reason as the one above. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".SlipReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          What the system wakes when a picture is added, while slips are
          switched on. Exported because the one that binds it is the system,
          like BootReceiver's sender; BIND_JOB_SERVICE is a permission only the
          system holds, so nothing else can start it.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".SlipWatchJob"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_JOB_SERVICE" />

        <!--
          Money coming in, read off K PLUS's and Krungthai NEXT's notifications.
          Nothing reaches it until the person allows it on the phone's own
          settings page. BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE is a permission
          only the system holds, and not exported is how Android's own
          documentation declares a listener: the system binds it regardless,
          and nothing else has any business to.

          Android cannot be asked for two apps' notifications, only for kinds
          of notification, so the filter asks for as few kinds as it can: the
          ones that make a sound and the ones that do not, and never
          conversations - nothing from LINE or any other chat app - or anything
          ongoing. Listed as disabled, those two are shown switched off on the
          settings page and cannot be switched on there by mistake. Everything
          else that arrives is dropped on its package name, before anything
          else about it is read (BankNoticeListener.kt).
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".BankNoticeListener"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="Reup · เงินเข้า"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.service.notification.default_filter_types"
                android:value="alerting|silent" />
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.service.notification.disabled_filter_types"
                android:value="conversations|ongoing" />
        </service>

        <!--
          The widget on the home screen (ReupWidget.kt). Not exported: what
          sends to it is the system - adding, resizing, the clock or the zone
          being changed - and this app's own alarm and redraws, and the system
          reaches a receiver that is not exported. Nothing else has any
          business making it draw.

          The time, zone and language broadcasts are among the few the system
          still delivers to a receiver named in a manifest, and each one moves
          something the widget says: the date, which day "พรุ่งนี้" is.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".ReupWidget"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="Reup วันนี้">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIME_SET" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIMEZONE_CHANGED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.LOCALE_CHANGED" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/reup_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <!-- The round button on a note's corner, on the widget: takes the note
             off. Not exported, for the reason DoneReceiver is not. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".PeelReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          Writing a note, over the home screen: translucent, so the home screen
          stays behind it; its own task and out of recents, so sticking a note
          up does not bring the rest of the app forward. Not exported.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".AddNoteActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="แปะโน้ต"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize|stateVisible" />

        <!--
          Exported, because the sender is the system rather than this app. All
          three of these are protected broadcasts that only the OS is allowed to
          send, so exporting costs nothing.

          directBootAware is what lets LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED arrive at all — it
          is delivered before the user has unlocked after a restart, when a
          normal component is not yet running.

          That used to be free, because the receiver read a hardcoded list. It
          reads the database now, and the database is credential-encrypted,
          which is the right kind for a file holding medication times and money
          and the kind that does not exist yet at that point in a boot. So the
          receiver checks isUserUnlocked and returns if it is not; BOOT_COMPLETED
          does the work a moment after the first unlock. The declaration stays
          because on a phone with no secure lock screen the user is already
          unlocked when this arrives, and the early reschedule still happens.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".BootReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true"
            android:directBootAware="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          The wallpaper (ReupWallpaper.kt): the clip picked in Settings, playing
          behind the home screen. Exported, as every wallpaper is, because the
          system is what binds to it; BIND_WALLPAPER is a permission only the
          system holds, so nothing else can.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".ReupWallpaper"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/wallpaper_name"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_WALLPAPER">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.wallpaper.WallpaperService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.service.wallpaper"
                android:resource="@xml/reup_wallpaper" />
        </service>
    </application>
</manifest>
